Q. What is placement group?
Ans : 
Placement Group helps you decide how your EC2 instances are arranged to improve performance, availability, or fault isolation.
AWS provides 3 main types of Placement Groups.
1. Cluster Placement Group      -  Places instances close together in a single Availability Zone to support low-latency, high-throughput networking.
2. Spread Placement Group       -  Places EC2 instances on different physical hardware so that if one hardware fails, all instances are less likely to fail together. 
3. Partition Placement Group    -  Divides instances into logical partitions, with each partition using separate sets of racks.
4. When you launch instances in advance option you can find the placement group option.

Q. What are the limitation of placement group?
Ans :
1. Placement group name must be unique within your AWS account for the region.
2. You cannot merge placement group.
3. One instance launch in one placement group.
4. You cannot launch dedicated hosts in placement group.

Q. What is tenancy?
Ans : 
1. Tenancy decides whether your EC2 instance runs on shared hardware or dedicated hardware.
2. There are 3 main tenancy options for EC2 instances.
  a. Shared Tenancy (Default)    -    A normal EC2 instance using the default tenancy option.
  b. Dedicated Instance          -    only your instances runs and AWS has it's control.
  c. Dedicated Host              -    only your instances runs and ypu have it's control.

3. When you launch instances in advance option you can find the tenancy option.

